- How to handle equal costs and tie-breaking?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests deriving a solvable DP for contiguous partitions with a length-based cost, plus a linear-time optimization and path reconstruction.

## Key Concepts

- Contiguous partition DP
- Prefix sums and O(nk) optimization
- Backtracking to report segment endpoints

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxTotalWithCost(A, k, c) {
  const n = A.length;
  const S = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) S[i] = S[i-1] + A[i-1];
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
    let bestVal = -Infinity, bestIdx = -1;
    for (let i = t; i <= n; i++) {
      const j = i - 1;
      if (dp[j][t-1] > -Infinity) {
        const cand = dp[j][t-1] - S[j] + c * j;
        if (cand > bestVal) { bestVal = cand; bestIdx = j; }
      }
      if (bestVal > -Infinity) {
        dp[i][t] = S[i] - c * i + c + bestVal;
        par[i][t] = bestIdx;
      }
    }
  }
  const segments = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  if (dp[n][k] <= -Infinity/2) return {best: -Infinity, segments: []};
  while (t > 0) {
    const j = par[i][t];
    segments.push([j+1, i]);
    i = j; t--;
  }
  segments.reverse();
  return {best: dp[n][k], segments};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt if c is paid only for non-first element or per segment boundary?
- How would you extend to maximize total value for at most k segments instead of exactly k?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to generalize DP with an extra resource dimension and reconstruction, a common pattern in grid path problems with constraints. It also probes edge-case handling when i or j is at 0 and when t=0. 

## Key Concepts
- DP with an extra dimension for resource usage (K diagonals)
- 3-way transitions and path reconstruction
- Time/space trade-offs and potential optimizations

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostPath(grid, K) {
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const INF = 1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array.from({ length: m }, () => Array(K+1).fill(INF)));
  const parent = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array.from({ length: m }, () => Array(K+1).fill(null)));
  dp[0][0][0] = grid[0][0];
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < m; j++) {
      for (let t = 0; t <= K; t++) {
        if (i === 0 && j === 0 && t === 0) continue;
        let best = INF, pick = null;
        if (i > 0 && dp[i-1][j][t] < best) { best = dp[i-1][j][t]; pick = [i-1, j, t]; }
        if (j > 0 && dp[i][j-1][t] < best) { best = dp[i][j-1][t]; pick = [i, j-1, t]; }
        if (i > 0 && j > 0 && t > 0 && dp[i-1][j-1][t-1] < best) { best = dp[i-1][j-1][t-1]; pick = [i-1, j-1, t-1]; }
        if (best < INF) {
          dp[i][j][t] = best + grid[i][j];
          if (pick) parent[i][j][t] = pick;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // find best end state
  let bestCost = INF, endT = 0;
  for (let t = 0; t <= K; t++) {
    if (dp[n-1][m-1][t] < bestCost) { bestCost = dp[n-1][m-1][t]; endT = t; }
  }
  // reconstruct path
  const path = [];
  let i = n-1, j = m-1, t = endT;
  while (i !== 0 || j !== 0 || t !== 0) {
    path.push([i, j]);
    const p = parent[i][j][t];
    if (!p) break;
    [i, j, t] = p;
  }
  path.push([0,0]);
  path.reverse();
  return { cost: bestCost, path };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to reduce space to O(m K) using rolling arrays?
- How would you handle negative costs or unreachable endpoints?

